{
 "id": "2024-wan-flower",
 "name": "Flower Border Colours: pleasure rating of 24 photos",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original photographs: 24 photographs of flower borders (perennial plantings) taken by one of the authors in urban green spaces, with lawn and sky removed so that each border stands on a black background, chosen by colour richness (low, slightly low, slightly high, high) and colour harmony (monochrome warm, monochrome cool and neutral, bicolour red-orange and yellow-green, bicolour orange-yellow and blue-violet, mixed colour cool and neutral, mixed colour warm). Participants first give gender, occupation and education, as in the paper, read the paper's meaning of emotional pleasure (how much you enjoy viewing the flower border in the picture right now) and rate each of the 24 pictures on the paper's 5-point scale from 1 = very unhappy to 5 = very happy. Rebuilt from Wan, Shen, Cai, Su, Ren & Xia (2024), How to Make Flower Borders Benefit Public Emotional Health in Urban Green Space: A Perspective of Color Characteristics, Forests 15(10): 1688, with the photographs cropped from its Figure 1 and the rating question from its Section 2.3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: b01-b24 = images 1#-24# of Figure 1; answers are in pleasure, keyed by image id; the colour harmony and richness class of each image is stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: mean pleasure per image, correlated with the images' HSV colour characteristics (hue shares, saturation and value levels, number of colours and plant patches), factor analysis of the correlated characteristics and a weighted evaluation model; the paper (281 valid online questionnaires, mostly aged 18-55, plus a separate facial-expression experiment with 60 people) found 13 colour characteristics related to pleasure, with blue, neutral purple and low saturation positive, summarised as low-saturation blue-violet share, colour diversity, bright red share and base green share. Differences from the original study: (1) only the online pleasure questionnaire is replicated; the paper's lab experiment that recorded facial expressions with face recognition software is not; (2) the gender, occupation and education questions, the instruction text and the wording of the rating question are ours (the paper prints only the definition of emotional pleasure and the scale ends), and the paper does not state the order of the pictures, so here they are shown one at a time in individually randomized order; (3) the image number labels printed in the figure were covered in black, matching the background; (4) the photographs are the figure copies (about 700 px wide), not the 3936 x 2214 px originals.",
